Template: remotedesktop-server/database-provider
Type: select
Choices: sqlite, postgres, none
Default: sqlite
Description: Database for the Remote Desktop server:
 Device codes are kept in this database. SQLite is a file on this
 machine and needs no other service. PostgreSQL is an existing server
 whose connection string you will be asked for next.
 .
 "none" keeps the codes in memory only: every restart gives every
 device a new code, so unattended machines stop being reachable. Choose
 it only for a test server.
 .
 The monitoring console uses the same provider. With "none" it keeps
 its own SQLite file, because it cannot run without a database.

Template: remotedesktop-server/database-path
Type: string
Default: /var/lib/remotedesktop-server/rendezvous.db
Description: SQLite database file:
 The file the device codes are kept in. Its directory is created and
 made writable by the service account. The monitoring console keeps
 ops.db in the same directory.
 .
 Keep this on a local disk: SQLite corrupts on a network share. Back
 the file up; losing it gives every device a new code.

Template: remotedesktop-server/postgres-connection
Type: password
Default:
Description: PostgreSQL connection string:
 For example:
  Host=127.0.0.1;Port=5432;Database=remotedesktop;Username=remotedesktop;Password=...
 .
 The database itself must already exist; the server creates its
 tables. The monitoring console uses the same connection.
 .
 Leave this empty on an upgrade to keep the connection string the
 machine already has.

Template: remotedesktop-server/tls-mode
Type: select
Choices: generate, existing, none
Default: generate
Description: Signaling TLS:
 "generate" creates a self-signed certificate on this machine and
 serves HTTPS with it. Clients have to trust that certificate; the
 client and host packages can import it at install time.
 .
 "existing" uses a certificate you already have: a PKCS#12 (.pfx or
 .p12) file, or a PEM certificate with its key next to it.
 .
 "none" serves plain HTTP. Device codes and relay tickets then travel
 in the clear; only choose it behind a TLS reverse proxy or on a
 trusted network.

Template: remotedesktop-server/tls-certificate
Type: string
Default:
Description: Certificate file:
 A PKCS#12 file, or a PEM certificate whose private key is in a file
 with the same name and a .key extension next to it. The file is copied
 into /etc/remotedesktop-server/tls/custom and made readable by the
 service account.

Template: remotedesktop-server/tls-password
Type: password
Default:
Description: Certificate password:
 The password of the PKCS#12 file. Leave empty if it has none or the
 certificate is PEM.

Template: remotedesktop-server/tls-hostname
Type: string
Default:
Description: Public host name:
 A DNS name (or address) clients use to reach this server. It is added
 to the generated certificate, together with this machine's own name
 and addresses. Comma-separated names are accepted.
 .
 Without this, a client connecting through a public DNS name or a NAT
 address rejects the certificate: the name it asked for is not in it.
